Z||000032||Ken Wittlief||08-28-2001||02:12 PM||||I agree on the military stuff - its human nature, when we see or experience something we HAVE to put it somewhere in our mental model of the universe - we CANT see something, have no idea what it was, and simply walk away shaking our heads and going on with live as usual - if we did we wouldnt be able to function<P> so we have to catigoize everything somehow - and Im sure there are a lot of people out there that see new military stuff, have no idea what the current state of technology really is, and can only comprehend it as alien or other.<P>  when the US nuked Hiroshima, the people that survived in the city thought they had been hit with some kind of gasoline bomb, cause everyone was badly burned, and they had no concept of nuclear weapons - so the word spread the US planes had sprayed gasoline into a mist over the city, and then ignited it.<P>  personally I have also experienced things that could only be explained as paranormal or spiritual.||||reg||10
Z||000033||rogergarrison||08-28-2001||02:35 PM||||There is also an area to the southwest of Chillicothie Ohio that used to be similar to "area 51". It was on a smaller scale but was operated by Wright-Patterson AFB Foreign Technologies Division. It was all fenced and posted 'Top Secret Area' as Area 51 and used to even be on flight maps marked as 'restricted area, no flights permitted' For you that fly, it was not the MOA area, but an actual site. Some of the buildings are still there, but signs are still there even though it appears to be abandoned. At the time is was legitimatly in operation there were many residents in the area reporting aircraft with strange behavior. Sorry, but I cant remember what is was called.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||DaRkLoRD||08-28-2001||02:55 PM||||Roger, almost all the accounts I've read of the actions taken after the Roswell crash, indicates that the bulk of the recovered debris was taken to WP AFB for temporary storage and analysis. The best documented account of this was in a book written by Philip Corso, who was assigned the task of distributing recovered technology to various companies as "seed" technology to spur on human development of new devices. the book is titled "the day after roswell", and is very interesting..<BR>Corso was part of the Foreign Technologies Division, it was under his command for 4 years.<P>btw, I saw the "alien autopsy" video on Fox a few years ago, and I thought it looked a bit fake.. but, I could be wrong.<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000035||Ken Wittlief||08-28-2001||02:56 PM||||one of the military technologies that would blow you away if you saw it is unmanned jet aircraft.<P>  current fighter jets are limited in the manuvers they can make because a pilot can only pull so many Gs before he blacks out.<P>take the pilot out of the jet and it can be designed to pull many  more Gs. If you saw an aircraft at a distance that was flying away from you at, say mach 3, and it suddenly pitched straight up at, lets say, 30 Gs, from your perspective it would look like the plane was just barely moving one second, then it instantainously shot straight up.<P>  Anyone who saw such a thing would declare: "Nothing we have can fly like that!" nothing the general public is familiar with can - but Im sure there are classified unmanned aircraft that can.||||reg||10
Z||000036||JSocha||08-28-2001||03:09 PM||||Though I believe UFO's exsist I do feel that some abduction scenarios that people claim are or have been nothing more then just a form of "self-induced hypnosis".<P>Self-induced hypnosis can be experience in the interval between when you were awake and where you would actually be considered hypnotized by a professional.<P>At this point, is where you yourself can make subconsious suggestions and believe them.<P>You may have experienced this yourself when sleeping.  <P>Trying to get back to sleep, you are just dozing off...imagination is already in a the preliminary dream state (not quite awake/but not quite asleep)...and at some point, you feel you are awake, you feel your eyes are open (although they are not) cause you think you see what is around you, however, you can not move no matter how hard you try even if you think you are talking, but in fact are not.  <P>Perhaps you have even attempted to tell yourself to wake up and/or attempted to tell yourself to move...but in the end you feel all your attempts are in vain.  <P>You just can not snap out of it and when you do, you may have that feeling of being weighted down, groggy, sluggish and in some cases numb.<P>So in est, one could believe they are being upducted as your mind may subconsiously be setting you up for the evenings "Eye-lid Cinema" and this subconcious thought is just in the process of surfacing during that time.<P>Ever experience this yourself...or is this just me?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||7
Z||000037||DaRkLoRD||08-28-2001||03:21 PM||||JSocha, I've had the "pleasure" of experienciong sleep paralysis.. when you dream, your body is paralized by your brain so you don't act out your dream actions (sleepwalking is essentially a malfuction of this process....)<BR>anyway, once I woke up suddenly, but my body was still "frozen".. I couldn't even move my eyes.. they were open partway, and I was aware of what was going on.. I just couldn't move.. at first I was scared, then I remembered reading about sleep paralysis, so I calmed down... about 1/10000th of a percent..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I tried yelling.. in my mind I was screaming as loud as I could, but I wasn;t making any sound. after at least 2 minutes of this, I finally made a small noise, and shortly after that, it was like someone had let go of my body, and I could move it again.. for someone that didn't know what sleep paralysis was, that could be a terrifying experience..<BR>The state you refer to is called "theta", and is the only brain state which allows access to repressed or "lost" memories.. a trained hypnotherapist can induce this state, and retrieve memories.. or, while in this state, new "memories" can be constructed, and when they are recovered, they are indistinguishable from real memories of past events. If I ever have seen anything mysterious while half asleep, I usually consider it a dream or other hallucination, unless someone else saw the same thing, and reports the same details without being prompted or led by my description.. but as of yet, this hasn't happened.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>another thing, possibly totally unrelated.. I had foot surgery about 6 years ago, and just as I was losing consciousness in the operating room, I remember experiencing a wave of terror, when I the bright light shone down on my face.. later, I found out that I'd tried to sit up, and said "oh no, not again!" or something like that... I'm not sure what that means. and btw, this was long before I started watching x-files.. the next thing I remember after that, is vomiting all over a nurse after I woke up.. oops.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000038||JSocha||08-28-2001||03:35 PM||||During my knee surgery back in the early 80's, they put me under via the needle.  Even though I dozed off without really knowing it (as the operation was supposed to be a local but I was shaking so bad from the cold operating room and being naked they had to put me under) I could swear up and down that I was being hit by beams of bright intense light, which felt very, very hot, but didn't burn and it felt I was floating (not common to patients under anestia...which made me sicker then a dog and groggy for the next 3 days...low tolerance to drugs).<P>Then I started to awake in the recovery room, facing the ceiling and they had a small picture on the ceiling of an old red, wooden covered bridge over a river.  First thing I though was "How did I get hear?",  thinking I was awakening in another location after the bright hot lights, especially when I had forgot I was in surgery.<P>"Then one day at summer camp..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
