Z||000048||starfighter007@msn.com||08-28-2001||08:22 PM||||Jsocha....whach u b toeken bout Willis? <P> Well I've just talked to the person that gave me the information earlier today.I was concearned about where this took place the paper had stated that this took place in paradise valley arizona. And paradise valley is a littel village just on the other side of this area that we are reffering to Dreamy Draw dam by a good couple of miles at least 3miles. Well he informed me that when this took place it was part of the town of pardise valley and during this event the army /us goverment annexed this land from paradise valley. Although it is under the jurisdiction of the army corp of engineers.It was opened to the public to use as a park recreation area because the crash site was scowerd and then covered up with this earth dam. They have no fear of anybody finding anything that was left behind.<BR> Has anybody heard of what I assume is a book "Above top secret" written by Timothy Good ? ||||reg||1
Z||000049||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||01:06 AM||||If you think this through logically then the accounts we have of UFOs flying around, crashing, and people being abducted make no sense.<P> If these are spacecraft they must have come many lightyears from some other planet - would they send a ship that far with only 2 beings on it, or only 4?!<P>If they sent one or two and they crashed, wouldnt they send more?!<P>Would they buzz around and taunt people with their presence without establishing formal contact?!<P>Would they abduct beings who are obviously technologically advancing (humans) - perform medical experiments on us, and then let us go with no message or communication?!<P>Would they make contact with the military? or would they land at MIT or Cornell? Or would they land at the white house, or UN headquarters?!<P>If you lump together all the reports of alien encounters and sightings, and try to make sense of what they are up to - it doesnt make sense.<P>If they act in a manner that is not logical, scientific, or emotional, then we can never have any meaningfull interaction with them. If thats the case they might as well all go home and leave us alone.||||reg||10
Z||000050||Mach10||08-29-2001||01:49 AM||||MJ: I'm no pessimist. I'm a realist. Aside from that, though, I posted that example as an anecdote. If anyone wants for wait a couple of weeks, I'll get the articles scanned and posted. It really is a hoot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyhoo: to jump into the fray:<P>1) Aliens are real. C'mon. Does anyone REALLY believe that something so inherently simple (and ultimately complex) could only happen in ONE solar system!?! True, our configuration is rare. VERY rare. However, the Universe is VERY VERY BIG. There's LOTS of space to have comparatively rare solar systems with orbital configurations just like ours. Life MUST exist on other worlds. Inteligent life, also. <P>2) Having said that, I don't beleive that they are green, have an affinity for circular spacecraft, or go "Beep-beep-beep" and randomly probe people. Is it a coincidence that it's always the most unreliable sources that get "abducted?" If you're smart enough to zip Trillions of lightyears to earth, then SURELY you are smart enough to see the pattern, and GUESS that maybe the guy living in a trailer wouldn't be the best sample? And how much can you really learn about a species through the rectum?<BR>Ok, I'll stop with the cynicism...<BR>There have been a number of "unexplainable" events that have happened. There is Archaelogical evidence (well, it looks like it.) that things like this have happened in the past. I'm just not so sure that all of them originate from other worlds. Impossible? Probably not. Unlikely? Definitely. But then, as Terry Pratchett has said: "the chances of something so patently absurd as existing are millions to one... but million to one chances pop up 9 times out of 10"<P>3) Another Pratchett quote:<BR>"How can a government so famously inept at everything else that it does be suddenly so clever when it comes to coverups?"<BR>I don't think I need to say more. The military is always doing some crazy back-door shinanigans with stuff. They need a place to play with it. So they build a ruddy great concrete whatever in the middle of somewhere. How's that relate at all to UFOs? Seems that UFO sightings are usually near Army bases. Think Stealth Bomber. That was "unveiled" publicly during Desert Storm. They had to fly the thing sometime. It's entirely possible that the stealth fighter testing is responsible for at least some UFO sightings. Same goes with any number of experimental aircraft. <P>Of course, I'd probably change my tune if I'd actually SEEN something completely unexplainable. But until then, until Lodos and Kathos(?) come slavering at my door demanding my alegiance, I'll remain skeptical.<P>Oh, and I think that SETI@Home is a really cool idea. That's our best bet at finding anything really cool.||||reg||6
Z||000051||c m west||08-29-2001||02:27 AM||||Am I the only one that believes that it is time for the government to answer up - even if it meant that thier would be answers that we would not like?||||reg||1
Z||000052||Raydar||08-29-2001||09:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>Am I the only one that believes that it is time for the government to answer up - even if it meant that thier would be answers that we would not like?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't see that happening, at least for a while.<BR>I believe the government considers the general population to be irrational morons. They would be afraid that <I>we'd</I> lose control and that <I>they'd</I> lose control of <I>us</I>.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000053||JSocha||08-29-2001||10:12 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  How can one say they do not believe in aliens?  I believe strongly in them.  I am one...from Canada.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>See ---&gt; <IMG SRC="http://www.twinoaks4fun.com/smilies/baby08.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"> And no one can deny my very exsistance...Muuwahahahahahaha!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><P>Unless....of course...you...really...want to.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000054||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||11:19 AM||||To assume there MUST be life on other worlds is an error. There has been a lot of work done that shows the complexity of DNA, and even below that, the complexity of simple protiens that are necessary for all forms of life that we know of.<P> they are so complex that the chance of them happening by randon is so extreemly slim, that given the age of the universe and the number of atoms in the entire universe, then according to statistics there has not been enough time since the big bang for enough of the possible combinations to have happened at random yet, not even once - not even close.<P>  So from a bilogy perspective, if life happens by random chance, then it is most likely that so far it has only happened once.<P>  People running the SETI program are like the idiot who wins the lottery - he goes out and buys one ticket and wins millions of dollars - from his perspective, it was easy, all he did was buy one ticket - so everyone should quit their jobs and buy a lottery ticket, cause it happened to him so it will happen to everyone.<P>  SETI says there are thousands of earth like planets in our galaxy, but biologist say there most likely is only one planet in the entire universe on which life has 'happened'.<P>  Ignoring the religious aspects of this, from a scientific point of view the conclusions are profound. We most likely are all alone in the universe.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 08-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000055||JSocha||08-29-2001||11:47 AM||||If we were to buy into that possibility of being the ONLY life form in exsistance (intelligent or not), then that subsequently supports the arguement that we as a species are even more arogant then what we ourselves personally believe we are.<P>Space is infinate to our current knowledge and that we know of.  The incetecimal universes that we are currently aware of and there planets, may or may not hold other life forms.  At this point, we will never know because they are out of reach.<P>Perhaps, some other planets are still in the stone trowing and evolutionary phase of their exsistance while others are far more technologically advanced that they can visit our planet at will, but we perhaps are too primitive for them, or borrowing from Star Trek for a moment, perhaps they themselves have a prime directive "To not interfere" as it may upset some sort of balance, that we ourselves may not be aware of from possible past experiences that they may have had.<P>And, as a side note, a well known mathmatical scientist and/or astrologer came up with a mathimatical formula to show that the odds are greater then what we as an already senctiant life form are willing to phanthom.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000056||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||12:08 PM||||yes, the Drake equation has been used by SETI to justify their position, that there MUST be thousands of advanced civilizations in our galaxy alone.<P>  but the error in the application of Drakes equation is exactly what I pointed out in my last post, they assume the probability of life 'happening' on a planet that can sustain it is close to 1 - they assume since life happened here, it will happen everywhere it can.<P> but biologist have determined the complexity of protiens alone that life is based on is of the order of 10^-380 or so (im recalling from memory) - so if you take the elements that are present in protien and throw them together the odds that they will randomly form a protien that can sustain life is 0.0[insert 379 zeros here]1 to 1 against it.<P>  there has not been 10^380 seconds since the big bang - and there is not 10^380 atoms of matter in the ENTIRE Universe!!!<P>  funny thing about protien is, if you get the elements wrong, it is lethal - it not only cant live - it kills living things<P>  I dont know why you say the universe is infinite? Astromeners belive space is finite, the amount of matter is finite, and that time itself did not exist before the big bang.<P>  Im not leaning towards one moral, religious, or scientific direction here for any personal preference - If biology and astronomy indicate we are the only living things in the universe then it has a very profound message for the human race as to what our purpose is, and to what our destiny is. If we are the only life that exists, then we have a lot of work to do!||||reg||1
Z||000057||lou_dias||08-29-2001||12:10 PM||||What's the best way to communicate with a violent, immature, primitive warlike race that is constantly excercising genocide?<P>You put a couple of guniea pigs in the middle of nowhere with no way to defend themselves (crash a ufo on purpose) and watch what happens.<P>Supposedly for Roswell, 2 ufo's collided during a thunder and lightning storm.  Since the theory behind ufos is that they use electic fields to propell themselves it's possible that such a storm caused a crash, but it could have been intentional too.<BR>They were defenseless but alive so we took them, studied them and established communications.<P>Notice how ufo sightings skyrocketed after we detonated the first nuclear bomb.  We finally showed up on their "radar" as being able to pose a potential threat.  Notice how in the past 50 years despite the fact that computers have become a million times more powerful, we still can't get off this planet easily and the main form of "efficient" transportation is still the internal combustion engine which was invented in what 1790?<P>We have an agreement to stay on this planet for a while, + our economy would be ruined if we had free energy.  Religion would go out the door too if aliens existed...  Then what would all those wackos have to believe in.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000058||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||12:11 PM||||BTW there is something about the Prime Directive from S.T. that makes it absurd - I cant remember it at the moment - let me stew on it and it will come back to me.||||reg||1
Z||000059||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||12:23 PM||||how would free energy ruin our economy?! I look at my monthly budget, less than 2% is spent on elect, gas or fuel.<P>aliens would absoluty have to explain their beliefs on the origins of life - they would have to account for who and what they are. And there is plenty of info in several religions that would peg 'aliens' as evil spirits who have come to mislead and destroy us.<P>  All that would have to be sorted out. After Uri took the first human flight into space, he said in one press conference that he had now flown into the heavens, and he didnt see God anywhere [the USSR was against all religions].<P>  Billy Grahm heard about this and replied, if he had stepped outside his space capsule, then he would have seen God.<P>  The social, economic, moral, scientific, and religious impacts of any milestone MUST be dealt with and faced squarely as they arise. Aliens would have to explain their views on our religions, how they fit into the big picture, and we would have to decide for ourselves to accept or reject them and their views, ideas, culture, science, morals...<P>  none of this is changed by keeping their presence a poorly guarded secret from the general public. Religion and cultures have existed for thousands of years. Turn on your nightly news. Look at what is going on in the middle east. Do you see any signs of religious beliefs going away any time soon?<P>  Did the ban on religion in the USSR put an end to religious beliefs there, or did it put an end to the USSR?||||reg||1
Z||000060||JSocha||08-29-2001||12:31 PM||||Bare with me...<P>RNA/DNA? Protein? Lipids? Carbon? Nitrogen? O2? H20? etc...<P>I agree, that these are the fundemental building blocks of our planet and as we know them.  Through some strange way and cosmic mixture and mitigating circumstances we have been able to sustain life as WE know it on our planet.  And this is due to the labels we have put on the current genetics, etc., to define them so we understand what we are talking about.<P>However, there could be other unknown atoms, chemicals, atmospheres, materials and circumstances to which we are unaware of that can in fact create and support life on another planet.<P>I feel we assume that all life must have these building blocks to sustain life.  Heck, DNA here might be called SNA (Sub-nucleic Amniotides) on some other planet, be somewhat similar to our twisting ladder DNA, but instead of 2 sides has a 3 center strand and is bound by rungs from the center out to each side.<P>In addition, atmosphere with its O2, CO2, etc. supports us above water while fish have adapted to underwater life, again as we know it.<P>Even fish and other aquatic organisms have been found to live on the extreme floor bed depths of our oceans in very harsh environments, next to volcanic vents, in extreme noxious and severe heat conditions and can survive where any other aquatic species would die instantly if even a small portion of that was immidately introduced into their environment to which they reside.<p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 08-29-2001).]||||reg||1
