Z||000062||lou_dias||08-29-2001||01:28 PM||||My point was total system shock.<BR>2% of everybody's income is still alot of money.  When you spend $20/week on gas (if you don't own a truck) and $50 a week on electricity and $100/month on Gas/home heating, I don't know about you, but that's more than 2% of my budget and somebody is getting rich off of it...<P><BR>I think they are called:  MOBIL, TEXACO, CHEVRON, Quaker State...<P>Big oil companies lobby against alternative fuel because it means they have to spend money on research.  Research is a big loss unless you actually come out with another money maker do to research.  That's why most research is done at Universities.<P>I went to a lecture at a local community college. (Bristol Community College in Fall River) about 10 years ago.  The guest speaker was a former atomic engineer for Westingate or some old big company that just does government work and he went on to say that they already has a fision (not fusion or is it the other way around) engine and showed us a picture of both and they were from the 70's I think.  He described the fision engine as being 3' x 12' and had the power to propel something the size of the space shuttle across the galaxy if needed.  Stan Friedman is his name.  He's a big UFO specialist that alot of the special presentation UFO shows interview.  He only talks about facts and has written several books.||||reg||1
Z||000063||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||01:47 PM||||im not surprized that you are surprized! :c)<P>people tend to believe what they want to believe, and then look for facts to support their postion. <P>When Drake came up with his equation there was  a lot of good science to support some of the factors - how many stars are in the galaxy - how many of those are like our sun - how many might have planets around them, how many will be earth like - exellent science went into those parts of it<P> but when they concluded there were some 10,000 or so planets in the galaxy that could sustain life, and had to decide on how many of those planets did life 'happen' into existance - they blew it! They threw up their hands and said "All of them!!!" They put absolutely no biology behind their number.<P>  and they had to! if there are only 10,000 planets in our galaxy that can sustain life, then any serious odds against spontaionious life makes their answer come out to be zero!<P> if they said the odds are 1 out of 1000 of life happening, then there are only 10 sources of life in our galaxy - and the galaxy is a very large place - the likelyhood of contacting any of the 10 would be near zero and SETI would be dead!<P> The biologist that are working on the complexity of life, and the chance of it 'happening' by itself, are not interested in astronomy or SETI. They are trying to determine how life could sring into existance on its own, and how it can evolve from there.  The fact that the complexity is 10E380 in magnitude has never bothered them, cause they assume all they need is alot of time and a lot of matter and eventually it will happen.<P>Kinda like Jshosa wants to assume there are other elements and other forms of matter, and other patterns in which elements can generate life - even though every single living thing on earth has DNA.<P>Isnt that like loosing your job and saying, there are an infinite number of employers out there that need workers, so I dont even have to look for a job, one will find me?!<P>  It never occurred to them to actually check with the astronomers and see how much time has elasped since the big bang, or how much matter is in the universe.<P>  So some bright guy up in NY puts these two things together and, yes the result is surprizing! Its shocking! Its profound!<P>  We have reached a point where we can look at the galaxy and grasp a concept of what is out there. And we can look at ourselves and understand the complexity of life. If we are honest with ourselves and belive what our science is telling us, then mankinds destiny becomes clear:<P>  From a strictly scientific perspective we are the result of an extreemly rare random event in an un-populated universe. We are also very fragile and precariously balanced at the face of extinction.<P>  We have the knowledge and technology to spread life across out own solar system, and to nearby stars, and gradually over thousands, maybe millions of years, to spread life throughout the galaxy<P>  maybe even throughout the universe. ||||reg||1
Z||000064||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||02:10 PM||||BTW as far as the US government is concerned, SETI IS DEAD!<P>they stopped funding it quite a while ago - completely!<P>so maybe Im not the first one to put the two sets of numbers together and realize there is nobody out there to listen to with a giant antenna.<P>Also, in recent years, NASA has stepped up its research into means by which interstellar travel can be accomplished - not Star Trek Warp speed stuff, a trip to a nearby star might take a hundred years of transit time, but it is within our reach.||||reg||1
