Z||000067||DRH||08-29-2001||02:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B><BR>  We have the knowledge and technology to spread life across out own solar system, and to nearby stars, and gradually over thousands, maybe millions of years, to spread life throughout the galaxy<P>  maybe even throughout the universe. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You just hit on the reason why there could be other life out there even if life only spontaineously occured once in the whole universe. We live in a relatively young solar system in a relatively young galaxy. Who's to say we aren't the result of a civilization <B>billions</B> of years older spreading the seeds of life?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000068||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||02:53 PM||||DRH you hit the most likely answer.<P>if there are 10,000 earth like planets in our galaxy then the chance of life happening on THIS one is 1 out of 10,000!<P>so from a statistics perspective it is MOST LIKELY that life 1st happened on one of the other 10,000, and was transplanted here.<P>Which would make us colonist already - we colonized the earth.<P> But then, why dont we know this?! if we had the technology to come here from another star, then why dont we remember coming here? And why are we not already in contact with the rest of the other inhabited star systems?<P> and why are there no visits between us and the other colonies?<P> One possibility is that something catastrophic happened in our past that we did not record in history, and the records and history of us coming here has been lost.<P> but then why havent the other colonies sent another group to start over here? And why for the last 40 years, when the SETI people have been listening, do they hear nothing?<P> So yes, I agree - its possible we are the colonist already - but it raises a lot of unanswered questions - <P> but our situation remains the same. We are here. We have no contact with anyone. We have no evidence of anyone bringing us here.<P> so as far as we know, we are still all alone in the universe - and there is nothing we can do but set out from here and spread life as far as we can.<P>  or maybe that was the plan anyway - we were put here and once we gained enough technology, in isolation,  to make the trips, it was taken for granted that we would carry on the mission on our own, without the need to be told to do so.||||reg||1
Z||000069||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||02:59 PM||||its kinda like a computer game - they put a key somewhere, and they put a door somewhere, and they know if you wander around long enough you will find both, and open the door.<P> Our destiny is in our situation - we see the universe, we see the microscopic complexity of life, we see the laws of physics, we see we are vunerable here on one planet all alone<P>  We add it all up and start building starships!||||reg||1
Z||000070||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||03:30 PM||||Ive been trying to remember what it is about the Prime Directive on startrek that makes it logically absurd - its still escaping me.<P>but something like, the P.D. says you cant interfere with the 'evolution' or development of a planets inhabitants until they reach warp drive ability.<P>But the only law of evolution is survival of the fittest!<P>I cant put my finger on it right now, but the two are not compatible. Sitting by and watching a civilization struggle, suffer, and maybe become extinct, without interacting with it, makes an assumption that life is somehow evil - that if you inject truth, knowledge, technology into their existance before they are 'ready' for it, that you corrupt it and you spoil what it would evolve into on its own.<P>In a way its assuming that you are evil, and that you should not be out there corrupting the universe.<P>  But that is a moral judgment - that life is evil! What if the society is good - its people are benevolant? Then interjecting truth and technology into their socity will let them accomplish more positive things, will end suffering sooner, and will avoid the possible total loss of their existance.<P> And there is something else - to be on a mission to seek out new life and new civilizations, to be on a quest to expand your own understanding of the nature of the universe, but then to refuse to share it with the very people you have 'sought out and discovered' is hypocritical at best.<P> They make it sound like the prime directive is the opposite of conquest - but its not.<P> Im still missing something here - it will probabally come to me tonight at about 4am.||||reg||1
