Z||000073||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||03:57 PM||||It occured to me one day while watching a 'nature show' - does the camera crew really scout around for weeks at a time to catch the rare shot of a fox chasing down a rabbit in the snow, catching it and eating it?<P> or do they bring their own rabbits with them? and their own fox?! and some 20 lb test fishing string to make sure they dont waste alot of film.<P> the one that blew it for me was a program about the fires in the plains of africa that happen every 7 years, and how some plants need the fires... blah blah balh<P> and sure enough, in the next scene, the plain is on fire!<P> so what do you believe?<P>1. the camera crew sat around for 7 years waiting for a fire to break out?<P>2. They were incredibly lucky and a fire broke out during the 2 weeks they were there? [2 weeks out of 7 years is 1:182 odds!]<P>3. someone had a bic lighter in his pocket and knew how to use it!||||reg||1
Z||000074||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||04:35 PM||||getting back to the subject - this idea of us possibly being the only life in the universe is something i have been thinking about for a while now<P> it has a lot of implications for humanity<P>  and there are a lot of things that would have to be worked out before we can set out in starships for the nearest stars<P> but I have an intuitive feeling that there is something to this, something big - the potential for science meets religion meets ecology meets biology meets genetics meets culture<P>  and humanity meets its destiny - I think its much more interseting than almost everything else that is going on in the world right now.<P> and I think it has the potential to pull people together across cultures and national borders to focus on a common goal.||||reg||1
